# minimum required Qore version
%requires qore >= 2.0
%modern

module QoreCrypto {
    version = "1.1.0";
    desc = "user module providing generic envelope encryption helpers";
    author = "Qore Technologies, s.r.o.";
    url = "http://qore.org";
    license = "MIT";
}

/** @mainpage QoreCrypto Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section qorecryptointro QoreCrypto Module Introduction

    The %QoreCrypto module provides a small provider-neutral envelope
    encryption API for sensitive long-lived records.  It uses existing Qore
    cryptographic primitives and provides:

    - AES-256-GCM content encryption with mandatory Additional Authenticated
      Data (AAD)
    - per-record data-encryption keys (DEKs)
    - key-provider-managed wrapping of DEKs
    - a versioned ciphertext envelope suitable for YAML/JSON serialization
    - deterministic canonicalization for structured AAD
    - key rotation by rewrapping DEKs or re-encrypting records
    - keyed blind indexes for equality lookups over encrypted columns
    - bounded retry and a fail-closed circuit breaker around key providers

    @par Example
    @code{.py}
%modern
%requires QoreCrypto

StaticKeyProvider provider(get_random_bytes(32));
EnvelopeCipher cipher(provider);

hash<CiphertextEnvelope> env = cipher.encrypt(
    "secret text",
    {"table": "qonsole_chat_messages", "record_id": "msg-1"}
);

string plaintext = cipher.decryptToString(
    env,
    {"record_id": "msg-1", "table": "qonsole_chat_messages"}
);
    @endcode

    @section qorecrypto_aad AAD Is Mandatory

    Every envelope is bound to structured AAD that is authenticated but not
    encrypted.  AAD should identify the record: its logical table or
    collection, its record id, its tenant or instance scope, and a schema
    version.  Binding those fields means a ciphertext cannot be silently moved
    to a different row, a different tenant, or a different schema generation
    and still decrypt.

    AAD is canonicalized deterministically - hash keys are sorted recursively
    and every value is length-prefixed - so the same logical AAD always
    produces the same bytes regardless of hash insertion order.

    @subsection qorecrypto_aad_stability AAD Field Stability

    AAD fields must be stable for the life of the record.  If any AAD field
    changes - a primary key is resequenced, a tenant is renamed, a schema
    version is bumped - the record cannot be rewrapped; it must be decrypted
    under the old AAD and encrypted again under the new AAD with
    @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::reencrypt() "EnvelopeCipher::reencrypt()".
    Rewrapping across an AAD change produces a record that will never decrypt
    again.

    @section qorecrypto_rotation Key Rotation

    A ciphertext envelope records the key provider and the key generation that
    wrapped its DEK, which is what makes rotation possible after the fact.  A
    provider that reports a constant key id across KEK changes cannot be
    rotated at all: nothing in the stored data distinguishes one generation
    from the next, so there is no dual-read window and no way to tell which
    records still need work.  Providers must issue a distinct key id per KEK
    generation from the first record they write.

    There are two rotation paths and they are not interchangeable:

    |!Path|!Method|!Cost|!When
    |Rewrap|@ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::rewrap() "rewrap()"|key metadata only; content ciphertext is untouched and the record is never decrypted|a new KEK generation, or a move to a new key provider, with unchanged AAD
    |Re-encrypt|@ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::reencrypt() "reencrypt()"|full decrypt and encrypt under a fresh DEK|any AAD change, or a content-key compromise

    Rewrapping does not need the record AAD: the wrapping AAD is rebuilt from
    the AAD digest already stored in the envelope, so a rotation job can walk a
    table without reconstructing per-row AAD and without ever holding record
    plaintext.

    @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::needsRewrap() "needsRewrap()" reports
    whether a record is still on an older generation using envelope metadata
    alone, so progress can be measured cheaply over a whole table.

    A rotation keeps working only while retired generations remain available
    for unwrapping.  Construct the provider with a keyring covering every live
    generation, retire a generation from the keyring only once no record
    references it, and use
    @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::rewrapAcross() "rewrapAcross()" to move
    records between providers.

    @section qorecrypto_blind_index Blind Indexes

    @ref QoreCrypto::BlindIndex "BlindIndex" produces keyed HMAC-SHA256 tokens
    for equality lookups over encrypted columns, so a caller can find a row by
    an exact value without storing that value in plaintext.

    Blind index keys are separate from KEKs and rotate independently, because
    the costs differ sharply: rewrapping a DEK touches key metadata only, while
    rotating a blind index key means recomputing every stored token from
    plaintext.  Tokens carry the generation that produced them, so a rotation
    can proceed row by row while
    @ref QoreCrypto::BlindIndex::indexAll() "indexAll()" keeps lookups matching
    across every live generation.

    A blind index reveals equality, and therefore value frequency.  It is not
    suitable for low-entropy values whose distribution is itself sensitive, and
    it is never an authenticator.

    @section qorecrypto_resilience Key Provider Failures

    Key providers fail closed.  A record whose DEK cannot be unwrapped does not
    decrypt, and a write whose DEK cannot be wrapped does not fall back to
    storing plaintext.

    @ref QoreCrypto::ResilientKeyProvider "ResilientKeyProvider" wraps a
    provider with bounded retry, exponential backoff, and a per-key-generation
    circuit breaker, so that a provider outage fails fast instead of pushing
    its latency onto every caller.  An open circuit still fails closed: it
    throws \c QORE-CRYPTO-PROVIDER-UNAVAILABLE rather than degrading.

    Only transient failures are retried.  A deterministic cryptographic failure
    - the wrong key, mismatched AAD, a tampered record - is neither retried nor
    counted toward opening the circuit, so a single corrupt row cannot present
    as a provider-wide outage.

    @section qorecrypto_zeroization Zeroization Limits

    %Qore is garbage-collected, and plaintext DEK buffers cannot be reliably
    zeroized from %Qore code: a binary value may be copied by the runtime, and
    its memory is released on collection rather than at a point the caller
    controls.  This module therefore does not claim to zeroize key material.

    What it does guarantee is narrower and enforceable: plaintext DEKs and
    unwrapped key material are never logged, never serialized into an envelope,
    and never held in a long-lived cache.  A DEK exists only for the duration of
    a single encrypt, decrypt, or rewrap call.

    Callers that require guaranteed zeroization need a C++ implementation; that
    is out of scope for this module.

    @section qorecrypto_fips FIPS Posture

    This module performs no cryptography of its own.  AES-256-GCM, HMAC-SHA256,
    SHA-256, and random byte generation all come from %Qore core primitives,
    which use the OpenSSL build %Qore was compiled against.  A FIPS-constrained
    deployment is therefore a property of that OpenSSL build and of the key
    providers in use, not of this module.

    The algorithms selected here - AES-256-GCM for content and key wrapping,
    HMAC-SHA256 for blind indexes - are FIPS-approved, so no algorithm change is
    required for a FIPS deployment.  Provider backends that reach external key
    management systems must be assessed individually.

    @section qorecrypto_relnotes QoreCrypto Module Release Notes

    @subsection qorecrypto_1_1 QoreCrypto Module Version 1.1
    - added key rotation support:
      - @ref QoreCrypto::StaticKeyProvider "StaticKeyProvider" and
        @ref QoreCrypto::FileKeyProvider "FileKeyProvider" hold a keyring of KEK
        generations, so retired generations stay available for unwrapping
      - @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::rewrap() "EnvelopeCipher::rewrap()" and
        @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::rewrapAcross() "rewrapAcross()" rotate
        stored ciphertext without decrypting record content
      - @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::reencrypt() "EnvelopeCipher::reencrypt()"
        handles rotations that change AAD
      - @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher::needsRewrap() "EnvelopeCipher::needsRewrap()"
        reports rotation progress from envelope metadata alone
    - added @ref QoreCrypto::BlindIndex "BlindIndex" for equality lookups over
      encrypted columns, with independently rotatable keys
    - added @ref QoreCrypto::ResilientKeyProvider "ResilientKeyProvider"
      providing bounded retry with backoff and a fail-closed per-key circuit
      breaker
    - documented @ref qorecrypto_aad_stability "AAD field stability",
      @ref qorecrypto_zeroization "zeroization limits", and
      @ref qorecrypto_fips "FIPS posture"

    @subsection qorecrypto_1_0 QoreCrypto Module Version 1.0
    - Initial release with @ref QoreCrypto::EnvelopeCipher "EnvelopeCipher",
      @ref QoreCrypto::AbstractKeyProvider "AbstractKeyProvider",
      @ref QoreCrypto::StaticKeyProvider "StaticKeyProvider", and
      @ref QoreCrypto::FileKeyProvider "FileKeyProvider".
*/
